CONSTRAINED HYDRIC GOVERNANCE: A NATIONAL AI-GOVERNED WATER BALANCE ARCHITECTURE WITH CONSTITUTIONAL SAFEGUARDS FOR SEMI-ARID STATES

From Hydraulic to Cognitive Infrastructure — A Validated Theoretical Framework for Morocco


ouadi Maakoul 

---

ABSTRACT

Morocco's water crisis is structural: agriculture consumes approximately 88% of national water withdrawals on less than 20% of cultivable land, while aquifers in critical basins face projected depletion of 337 million m³ by 2050 under business-as-usual. In response, the National Office of Electricity and Drinking Water (ONEE) launched a nationwide digital platform in 2025–2026 to centralize real-time monitoring, SCADA integration, and intelligent alerting across its fragmented water infrastructure. This platform represents a critical sensing layer. It does not, however, constitute a cognitive governance architecture: it lacks predictive allocation, learning under strategic behavior, and constitutional enforcement of long-term hydric sustainability. Meanwhile, Morocco plans to produce 1.7 billion m³/year of desalinated water by 2030, all powered by renewable energy, fundamentally coupling water and energy governance.

This dissertation proposes to operationalize water as strategic infrastructure by designing a Constrained Hydric Governance (CHG) framework—a hierarchical AI-governed system that closes the loop from sensing and prediction to constitutional policy actuation. The contribution is threefold. First, a formal theory of constitutional computability for resource governance: sustainability principles encoded as machine-enforceable viability constraints within a constrained Markov decision process, drawing on control theory, viability theory, and safe reinforcement learning. Second, a hierarchical multi-agent allocation architecture tested on the Souss-Massa basin, where system dynamics models already forecast severe depletion and where UM6P's International Water Research Institute provides empirical baselines. Third, an institutional blueprint for an Autonomous National Water Authority (ANWA) with legally specified safe operating spaces, a game-theoretic delegation model, and a formal failure doctrine covering adversarial governance, cybersecurity, model drift, and constitutional override procedures. The framework is validated through a Minimum Viable Cognitive Twin for Souss-Massa and stress-tested against historical failure cases (Cape Town, Aral Sea, Ogallala, Jordan). The result is not a digital twin filing cabinet nor an AI-governance manifesto, but a rigorously constrained architecture for hydric sovereignty in climate-vulnerable states.

