CHAPTER 1: THE HYDRIC PRECIPICE AND THE COMPUTATIONAL IMPERATIVE

1.1 Structural Water Scarcity as a New Condition

Morocco has entered a phase of structural water scarcity that its own Minister of Equipment and Water, Nizar Baraka, has characterized as "no longer cyclical" but representing "a new stage requiring creativity, innovation, and intelligent governance." After seven consecutive years of drought, national dam reserves fell below 40% in 2025 before recovering to over 70% in early 2026 due to exceptional spring rainfall and snowmelt. This recovery, while welcome, masks systemic vulnerability: southern basins such as Souss-Massa and Draa continue to lag, evaporation losses claim approximately 30% of surface water annually, and groundwater extraction persistently exceeds renewable recharge in most agricultural basins.

National per capita water availability has dropped below 500 m³/year—the absolute water scarcity threshold. The agricultural sector, which generates approximately 14% of GDP but employs nearly 40% of the workforce, concentrates its water consumption on less than one-fifth of cultivable land, with export-oriented, water-intensive crops dominating. This structural imbalance between water endowment and economic allocation constitutes the foundational tension that any governance architecture must address.

1.2 The State's Ambitious Response: Sensing Without Cognition

The state's response has been ambitious. ONEE's national digital platform, launched in late 2025 and expanded through 2026, represents what its own documentation calls "a fundamental shift from fragmented oversight to unified supervision." The platform centralizes real-time data from treatment plants, reservoirs, and distribution networks, enabling intelligent alerts, historical cross-referencing, and system-wide infrastructure monitoring. It addresses a decades-old problem: "a patchwork of industrial automation equipment, remote management devices, control platforms, and geographic information systems that operate independently." The platform is designed to meet international cybersecurity standards (IEC 62443), protecting critical installations while ensuring reliable data exchanges between IT and operational technology environments.

The platform is conceived "as a strategic planning and decision-support tool, designed to centralize, analyze, and model key sector data to strengthen anticipation and governance of drinking water supply". The ambition is "to provide the country with a digital platform capable of centralizing, structuring, and making reliable all data from water supply and distribution networks". ONEE handles over 85% of drinking water production, using surface water, aquifers, and desalination plants like those in Agadir and Laayoune. The integration covers 41 groundwater intakes, 6 water treatment plants, 157 reservoirs, and 73 pumping stations.

Simultaneously, Morocco is executing one of the world's most aggressive desalination programs. Seventeen plants currently produce 350.3 million m³/year; four additional plants under construction will add 567 million m³/year by 2027; and eleven planned projects target a total national capacity of 1.7 billion m³/year by 2030—all powered by renewable energy. The Minister has stated that 60% of drinking water will come from desalinated seawater by 2030, up from 25% in 2025. A comprehensive integrated infrastructure program mobilizing nearly 130 billion dirhams (USD 13 billion) by 2030 couples water and energy investments.

These developments are significant. But they reveal a critical gap: ONEE's platform is a sensing and monitoring layer, not a cognitive governance layer. It visualizes and alerts; it does not learn, allocate, or enforce. It was not designed to optimize intersectoral water distribution under deep uncertainty, to model strategic farmer behavior in groundwater extraction, to embed sustainability constraints as constitutional objects, or to provide legally binding actuation. As Morocco's water system becomes increasingly digitized, centralized, and desalination-dependent, the absence of a constitutional governance architecture for AI-assisted resource allocation becomes the binding constraint on national hydric resilience.

1.3 The Core Argument

This dissertation argues that the next logical—and necessary—step is not more infrastructure, nor more sensors, but a Constrained Hydric Governance (CHG) architecture: a formally specified, legally embedded, AI-assisted governance system that keeps the nation's water balance within a constitutionally defined safe operating space. The argument is conservative in its ambitions: it does not propose that AI should govern water, but that constitutionally constrained computational governance systems—transparent, overridable, and formally verified—may improve the state's capacity to remain within hydrologically sustainable operating spaces under conditions of deep climate uncertainty and strategic resource competition.

1.4 Morocco's Historically Rare Position

Morocco occupies a historically rare position: centralized state capacity, accelerating digital infrastructure, severe climate pressure, manageable national scale, expanding renewable-powered desalination, an existing hydraulic megaproject tradition, and an active digital transformation underway at ONEE. This convergence creates what might be termed a "compressed governance modernization window"—an opportunity to build the constitutional architecture for algorithmic resource governance before the crisis removes all margin for deliberate institutional design. The framework proposed here, if validated, is generalizable to any semi-arid state confronting structural hydric deficit. The core insight—that sustainability constraints can be formalized as machine-enforceable viability conditions embedded within democratic constitutional orders—extends beyond water to carbon budgets, fisheries, and other existential resource commons. Water, as the most immediately binding constraint on human security in the climate era, is the appropriate place to begin.

