CHAPTER 5: THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK — CONSTRAINED HYDRIC GOVERNANCE

5.1 The Hydric State Vector and Safe Operating Space

The theoretical architecture begins with a formal representation of the water system. The hydric state vector at time $t$ is defined as:

\mathbf{H}_t = [S_{\text{surf}}, S_{\text{gw}}, SM, Q]

where $S_{\text{surf}}$ captures surface water storage (reservoirs, rivers), $S_{\text{gw}}$ captures groundwater storage across aquifers, $SM$ captures soil moisture, and $Q$ captures water quality parameters. The system evolves under climate forcing $\mathbf{C}_t$ (precipitation, temperature, evapotranspiration) and human control actions $\mathbf{U}_t$ (pumping allocations, reservoir releases, desalination activation).

The safe operating space $\mathcal{S}$ is defined as the set of states satisfying a vector of constitutional constraints:

\mathcal{S} = \{\mathbf{H} \in \mathbb{R}^n : g_i(\mathbf{H}) \leq 0, \; i = 1,\ldots,m\}

Constitutional constraints $g_i(\cdot)$ encode sustainability principles as machine-enforceable conditions. Examples include: (i) no basin's rolling three-year groundwater decline shall exceed 10% of mean renewable recharge; (ii) reservoir storage shall remain above dead pool with ≥95% probability over a five-year horizon; (iii) minimum human right to water allocation (per Morocco's Law 36-15, Article 2) shall be maintained in all regions; (iv) coastal aquifer piezometric levels shall remain above the seawater intrusion threshold.

5.2 Constitutional Computability: The Core Theoretical Innovation

The central theoretical contribution of this dissertation is constitutional computability: the translation of legal sustainability principles into machine-enforceable viability constraints that retain democratic legitimacy. This framework synthesizes three intellectual traditions.

Viability Theory (Aubin, 1991; Aubin, Bayen, and Saint-Pierre, 2011) provides the mathematical foundation. Viability theory "designs and develops mathematical and algorithmic methods for investigating the adaptation to viability constraints of evolutions governed by complex systems". The core concept is the viability kernel $\mathcal{K}$: the largest subset of the safe operating space $\mathcal{S}$ from which there exists at least one control policy that keeps the system within $\mathcal{S}$ indefinitely. If the hydric state enters a region outside the viability kernel, no feasible policy can prevent eventual constraint violation. The governance imperative is therefore to maintain the system within $\mathcal{K}$ at all times. The theory emphasizes "the construction of feedbacks and dynamical systems by myopic optimization methods"—a formulation that naturally accommodates the hierarchical, feedback-driven architecture of CHG.

Safe Reinforcement Learning provides the computational machinery. In a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP), the objective is to maximize expected cumulative reward subject to constraints on expected cumulative costs. Control barrier functions provide formal guarantees of constraint satisfaction by rendering the safe set forward-invariant: if the system starts within $\mathcal{S}$ and the control policy respects the barrier function condition, the system is guaranteed to remain within $\mathcal{S}$. The integration of viability theory's kernel concept with safe RL's barrier functions yields a framework where constitutional constraints are both mathematically rigorous and computationally tractable.

Algorithmic Constitutionalism provides the legal-theoretical legitimacy. Emerging scholarship argues that "algorithmic governance must rest on three principles: that all public power must be lawfully delegated through participatory authorization; that algorithmic decisions affecting rights must be contestable through independent review; and that constitutional values must constrain the design of algorithmic systems, not merely their outputs". This literature identifies a "governance gap" between rapid technological capability and slow-moving institutional wisdom. One framework proposes "Algorithmic Constitutionalism" as a legal framework designed "to address the unprecedented challenges posed by Artificial Intelligence in the displacement of power toward computational architectures," arguing that "classical constitutionalism is currently in a state of 'chronological lag'". Another contribution proposes a "Constitutional Architecture for Hybrid Societies" that couples "agency, authority, and civic learning through procedural feedback".

The CHG framework operationalizes these insights by specifying that constitutional constraints are encoded in the CMDP's barrier functions, that the Autonomous National Water Authority publishes its value function and constraint satisfaction metrics quarterly, that parliamentary override is available but triggers automatic review and mandatory aquifer compensation, and that the architecture includes a "Challenge Membrane" granting pause rights, bounded response windows, and independent escalation during algorithmic decision processes.

The relationship between constitutional computability and democratic legitimacy requires careful articulation. The framework does not claim that sustainability constraints can be derived algorithmically from constitutional text. Rather, it claims that once a constitutional principle is legislatively specified in quantitative terms (e.g., "the long-term sustainable yield of each aquifer shall not be exceeded"), the enforcement of that principle can be made computationally rigorous without violating democratic sovereignty—provided that override mechanisms, transparency requirements, and parliamentary accountability are embedded. The value function and constraint thresholds remain politically determined; the CHG architecture ensures they are machine-enforced.

5.3 Hierarchical Multi-Agent Governance

The allocation architecture is hierarchical, reflecting the nested structure of water governance in a unitary state. A national-level "Water Governor" agent sets basin-level extraction caps based on the hydric state vector $\mathbf{H}_t$ and constitutional viability constraints $\mathcal{S}$. Basin-level agents receive these caps and allocate water to irrigation districts, urban utilities, and industrial users. This two-level structure is modeled as a hierarchical CMDP where the national agent solves a constrained optimization over a longer horizon and coarser spatial resolution, while basin agents solve shorter-horizon, finer-resolution allocation problems subject to the national caps as hard constraints.

Farmers are modeled as strategic agents with heterogeneous objectives: export-oriented agribusiness maximizes irrigation certainty for high-value crops; smallholder farmers maximize livelihood stability; some agents may engage in illegal well drilling when enforcement is weak. The multi-agent reinforcement learning framework accommodates these strategic behaviors, including coalition formation (e.g., agricultural lobbies coordinating to resist extraction restrictions) and adaptive responses to policy changes (e.g., switching to more water-efficient crops when irrigation allocations decrease, or—perversely—expanding irrigated area when drip irrigation subsidies reduce per-hectare costs).

Multi-agent reinforcement learning has demonstrated significant potential in water environmental systems for "addressing cooperative control, policy optimization, and task allocation problems in complex systems". Recent work models water resource management as a common-pool resource environment for networked multi-agent reinforcement learning, where "agents in the networked control system use reinforcement learning to learn how to map from states to actions". Multi-objective reinforcement learning extends this to "handle multiple objectives simultaneously, yielding a set of policies that capture various trade-offs". The CHG framework advances this literature by embedding constitutional constraints as hard viability conditions rather than soft optimization targets, and by modeling the strategic interaction between the regulator and regulated agents rather than treating allocation as a centralized optimization problem.

5.4 Political Economy of Delegation

A game-theoretic model identifies the conditions under which political elites rationally delegate enforcement authority to an Autonomous National Water Authority. The model draws on the principal-agent logic of transaction cost economics, where "delegation by the government of regulatory powers to an independent agency is based on the principal-agent logic" and where "the theory of credible commitment suggests that independent (non-majoritarian) regulatory agencies are more effective in regulating markets than organizations under direct political control".

The game models two strategic actors: the political executive (Principal) and the ANWA (Agent, if delegation occurs). Under direct political control, the executive sets water allocation to maximize a utility function that includes both aggregate social welfare and private benefits to politically connected constituencies (e.g., export-oriented agricultural elites who provide political funding). Under delegation, the ANWA sets allocation to maximize constitutional sustainability subject to parliamentary oversight. The executive decides whether to delegate based on: drought severity (which increases the salience of long-term sustainability), elite asset exposure to water-dependent sectors (which determines whether elites benefit more from short-term extraction or long-term system viability), public pressure (which increases the electoral cost of visible water crises), and the credibility of constitutional commitment devices (which determines whether delegation is perceived as reversible).

The model predicts delegation when drought severity and public pressure are high, elite assets are sufficiently exposed to system collapse risk, and constitutional safeguards make the delegation credible. This prediction can be empirically calibrated against Morocco's 2020–2027 National Drinking Water Program, the 2025–2026 centralization of water data under ONEE's platform, and the 2011 constitutional reforms that strengthened independent institutions.

A critical finding from the commitment literature is that "a commitment by a political actor is only as good as it is credible—that is, if it is in the interests of the actor to keep it, possibly due to mechanisms put in place to induce just those commitments". This implies that the constitutional architecture must include self-enforcing features: mechanisms that make it costly for future executives to override ANWA decisions without triggering visible, politically damaging consequences (e.g., mandatory parliamentary review, public disclosure of constraint violations, aquifer compensation obligations).

5.5 Water-Energy Constitutional Coupling

Given Morocco's commitment to power all new desalination plants with renewable energy—and the target that 60% of drinking water will come from desalinated seawater by 2030—the governance architecture must formally couple water and energy systems. Desalination transforms an energy input into a water output; when both are governed by sustainability constraints, the coupling creates a constitutional feedback loop. If renewable generation falls short (e.g., due to extended low-wind periods), desalination output must be curtailed precisely when water demand may be highest (summer months). Conversely, if water scarcity triggers maximum desalination activation, grid stability may be threatened.

The extended state vector includes energy storage and renewable generation capacity. Desalination decisions become jointly constrained by water scarcity signals and grid stability conditions. The constitutional coupling prevents the emergence of a new vulnerability where hydric resilience is achieved at the cost of energy dependence—or where energy decarbonization is undermined by water sector energy demand. This coupling is particularly significant given that Mekorot in Israel, operating in a comparable desalination-dependent context, is "the single largest civilian energy consumer in Israel consuming 5% of the total amount of energy used in the country".

