CHAPTER 6: METHODOLOGY AND RESEARCH DESIGN

6.1 Phase 1: Dynamic Water Balance and Safe Operating Space Definition (RQ1)

The Souss-Massa basin covers approximately 27,000 km², bounded by the High Atlas and Anti-Atlas mountains, with mean annual precipitation declining from approximately 600 mm in the north to approximately 150 mm in the south. The basin has an estimated renewable water supply of 1,023 Mm³/year, with the Souss-Massa River Basin Agency (ABHSM) documenting annual abstractions that exceed renewable resources, producing a severe water deficit. National climate projections indicate average precipitation reductions of approximately 15% and groundwater recharge decreases of up to 27% in some sub-basins.

The methodology extends the UM6P system dynamics model as a baseline, then calibrates a stochastic dynamic water balance using CMIP6-downscaled precipitation and evapotranspiration ensembles. GRACE/GRACE-FO terrestrial water storage anomaly data is fused with in-situ measurements via the hybrid ensemble learning approaches validated in recent Moroccan studies (R² = 0.97). The safe operating space $\mathcal{S}$ is defined as a probabilistic viability set: a region of the state space where all constitutional constraints are satisfied with specified probability over specified horizons. The viability kernel is computed using barrier function methods, identifying the largest subset of $\mathcal{S}$ from which a feasible control policy exists.

Machine learning methods for GRACE downscaling have been validated across multiple contexts. A random forest model and artificial neural network model were developed to downscale GRACE data from 1° to 0.25° resolution. Deep learning methods using convolutional neural networks and generative adversarial networks, "trained with state-of-the-art numerical hydrology models," achieve further resolution enhancement. Soft constraint deep learning paradigms introduce "a novel loss function to obtain higher spatial resolutions while preserving the integrity of the GRACE TWSA signal". These validated techniques enable groundwater intelligence at resolutions relevant to basin-scale governance.

6.2 Phase 2: Hierarchical Multi-Agent Allocation under Constitutional Constraints (RQ2)

A two-level constrained Markov decision process is constructed. The national agent sets quarterly basin caps to maintain the hydric state vector within the safe operating space. The national agent's reward function includes both aggregate water productivity (economic output per cubic meter) and constraint satisfaction (penalties for approaching viability kernel boundaries). Basin agents receive these caps and allocate to user categories: irrigation districts, urban utilities, and industrial users.

Farmers are modeled as strategic agents using multi-agent reinforcement learning with opponent modeling. The agent population is procedurally generated to represent documented actor categories: export agribusiness (approximately 15% of farmers, consuming approximately 65% of irrigation water), smallholder farmers (approximately 70% of farmers, consuming approximately 25%), urban utilities (volume-stable demand, high priority), and the state (political stability objective). Agent utility functions are calibrated from peer-reviewed literature on Maghreb groundwater behavior, Moroccan agricultural census data, and expert elicitation from agricultural economists.

Reinforcement learning is justified over model predictive control (MPC) on the grounds that static optimization cannot handle strategic agent adaptation. MPC assumes the environment responds passively to control actions; in a multi-agent setting with strategic farmers who learn and adapt, the environment's response is itself the product of optimizing behavior. RL, used here as a governance simulator rather than a deployable controller, can model this co-adaptation and identify equilibrium outcomes under different constitutional constraint configurations.

6.3 Phase 3: Physics-Informed Aquifer Intelligence (RQ3)

A physics-informed neural network (PINN) is constructed, constrained by the groundwater flow equation. The network fuses three data streams: GRACE/GRACE-FO terrestrial water storage anomalies (monthly, native resolution 3°, downscaled to 0.04° using the validated random forest approach with R² = 0.92), Sentinel-1 Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (InSAR) subsidence data (6–12 day repeat, 20 m resolution), and in-situ well logs from ABHSM and partner institutions. The physics-informed constraint ensures that predictions respect conservation of mass and Darcy's law, preventing the network from learning spurious correlations that violate physical law.

The output is a weekly groundwater health index at 5 km resolution for the Souss-Massa basin, validated against the 337 million m³ depletion baseline from the UM6P system dynamics model. The PINN approach is specifically suited to data-scarce environments where in-situ measurements are sparse: the physical constraints regularize the learning problem, enabling reliable inference from limited observations.

6.4 Phase 4: Constitutional Architecture and Delegation Game (RQ4)

Constitutional constraints are formalized as barrier functions within the CMDP. For each constraint $g_i(\mathbf{H}) \leq 0$, a control barrier function $B_i(\mathbf{H})$ is constructed such that $B_i(\mathbf{H}) \geq 0$ when the constraint is satisfied, and the control policy is required to satisfy $\dot{B}_i(\mathbf{H}) \geq -\alpha B_i(\mathbf{H})$ for some $\alpha > 0$, ensuring forward invariance of the safe set.

The legal articles of the Strategic Water Constitution are drafted, specifying the ANWA's mandate, the appointment procedures for its governance board (with staggered terms to prevent executive capture), transparency obligations (quarterly publication of the value function, constraint satisfaction metrics, and any executive overrides with their justifications), and emergency override protocols. The override mechanism is designed to make constraint violations politically costly: a Prime Ministerial override triggers automatic parliamentary review within 30 days, mandatory public disclosure of the hydric state consequences, and a requirement that the override be compensated through alternative aquifer recharge measures or desalination activation within the fiscal year.

A Bayesian delegation game is constructed. Politicians choose between direct control and delegation to ANWA based on: drought severity (probability of multi-year drought), elite asset exposure to water-dependent sectors, public pressure (measured through protest frequency, media coverage, and electoral margins), and the credibility of constitutional commitment devices. The model is solved for Perfect Bayesian Equilibrium. It is calibrated using Morocco's 2020–2027 National Drinking Water Program investment data, elite agricultural asset concentration estimates, and drought frequency statistics from the historical record.

6.5 Phase 5: Adversarial Governance and Failure Doctrine (RQ5)

This phase provides a formal analysis of the failure modes of an AI-governed water system and the layered defense mechanisms required to maintain resilience. The analysis is organized around four threat categories.

Cyber-physical threats: SCADA intrusion, sensor spoofing, data poisoning, and optimization sabotage. Morocco's ONEE platform targets IEC 62443 compliance, but no standard is impenetrable. Research on "baiting AI" demonstrates a new cyber-attack vector targeting industrial control systems, "particularly focusing on water treatment facilities". The 2025 Norwegian dam cyberattack—where attackers remotely opened a valve—demonstrates the catastrophic potential of unsecured water infrastructure control systems. Defense mechanisms include Byzantine-robust state estimation (maintaining accurate hydric state estimates even when a subset of sensors is compromised), anomaly detection systems trained on normal operational patterns, and air-gapped fallback controllers.

Algorithmic failure modes: Goodhart's Law—the phenomenon where optimizing a proxy metric degrades the true objective—is a central concern. As one analysis notes, "as soon as we set a safety metric, agents (whether RL-trained or autonomous) optimize for the metric while hollowing out the actual intent". Model drift under non-stationary climate conditions can cause the learned allocation policy to become progressively suboptimal or even unsafe. Policy gaming by strategic agents—where farmers learn to manipulate the inputs to the allocation algorithm—can cause the system to allocate more water than sustainable. Hidden aquifer collapse undetected by sensors can proceed even while the AI reports constraint satisfaction. Defense mechanisms include multi-metric monitoring (tracking a diverse set of indicators, not a single optimization target), out-of-distribution detection (flagging states for which the model's predictions are unreliable), and regular re-calibration against ground-truth measurements.

Institutional failure modes: Regulatory capture, where the ANWA becomes dominated by the interests it is supposed to constrain. Constitutional suspension during crisis, where emergency powers are used to override sustainability constraints permanently rather than temporarily. AI lock-in, where path-dependent investment in a particular algorithmic architecture prevents necessary policy adaptation. Defense mechanisms include staggered board appointments, mandatory parliamentary review of any emergency override lasting more than 90 days, and a constitutional requirement that the AI architecture be periodically reviewed and, if necessary, replaced through a legislatively specified process.

Layered defense architecture: The failure doctrine specifies a defense-in-depth approach. At the sensing layer, Byzantine-robust estimation and anomaly detection protect data integrity. At the prediction layer, ensemble modeling and out-of-distribution detection protect against model drift. At the allocation layer, the CMDP's barrier functions provide formal guarantees of constraint satisfaction under normal operation. At the actuation layer, a rule-based fallback system activates when the AI layer is compromised, defaulting to conservative allocation rules based on historical averages and seasonal norms. The human override mechanism is constitutionally constrained: a Prime Ministerial override triggers automatic parliamentary review, public disclosure, and mandatory aquifer compensation, ensuring that overrides are politically costly and therefore credible as emergency measures rather than routine circumventions.

6.6 Phase 6: Minimum Viable Cognitive Twin and Validation (RQ6)

Phases 1–5 are integrated into a simulation environment for the Souss-Massa basin—a Minimum Viable Cognitive Twin. This is not a full national digital twin but a bounded, research-grade simulation that demonstrates proof-of-concept for the governance architecture.

The twin is stress-tested under five scenarios: (i) business-as-usual with ONEE platform only (sensing without enforcement), representing the current institutional trajectory; (ii) AI governance without equity constraints, representing a technocratic optimization that maximizes aggregate water productivity; (iii) AI governance with a Gini penalty, where distributional equity is explicitly penalized in the objective function, tracing the Pareto frontier between efficiency and equity; (iv) multi-year drought recurrence (2026–2030), representing the climate stress that Morocco has already experienced and will likely experience with greater frequency; and (v) adversarial cyber-physical attack scenarios, testing the resilience of the layered defense architecture.

Performance metrics include: aquifer recovery probability (the probability that groundwater levels stabilize or recover within a 20-year horizon), days of urban rationing (the frequency and duration of municipal supply interruptions), food import dependency (the proportion of staple food consumption met through imports), the Gini coefficient of irrigation access, and constraint violation frequency (how often constitutional constraints are violated under each governance regime).

Comparative validation benchmarks the CHG architecture against historical counterfactuals: Cape Town (what would have happened if a CHG-like system had triggered mandatory restrictions earlier), SGMA (how CHG's machine-enforceable constraints compare to SGMA's locally administered planning), and Jordan (whether a constitutional AI architecture could have prevented the emergence of the illegal water market).

