CHAPTER 7: ISRAEL'S MEKOROT — CENTRALIZED OPERATIONAL INTELLIGENCE AND ITS CONSTITUTIONAL LIMITS

Israel's national water company, Mekorot, provides the closest existing empirical benchmark for centralized, AI-augmented water management. A detailed examination reveals both the power of computational integration and the governance gap that CHG addresses.

Mekorot's VP of Technology, Shira Singer, has articulated the organizational vision with remarkable clarity: "Ultimately, the control room will resemble an 'autonomous vehicle.' The vast majority of optimal decisions will be made by artificial intelligence". This is not speculative futurism; it is the stated trajectory of an organization managing one of the world's most water-efficient national systems. Israel's water loss rate stands at "less than 4%, the lowest in the world," achieved through "advanced command systems, sensors, and cutting-edge technology, including artificial intelligence for predictive maintenance". The integration of SCADA systems, big data analytics, and machine learning enables "energy optimization in the operation of the water supply systems on the basis of the required and projected consumption".

The operational robustness of this system was demonstrated under extreme stress. "On the morning of October 7, when automated backup systems immediately activated, ensuring an uninterrupted water supply despite the nationwide chaos". This is a compelling demonstration that centralized, AI-augmented water infrastructure can maintain service continuity under conditions that would overwhelm conventional management.

Yet Mekorot's governance architecture reveals precisely the gap that CHG addresses. Mekorot is a state-owned utility operating under executive authority. Its AI systems optimize operational parameters—pump scheduling, leak detection, energy arbitrage—within a policy framework set by political authorities. They do not set sustainability constraints. They do not determine intersectoral allocation priorities. They do not enforce aquifer extraction limits against the wishes of politically connected agricultural interests. Mekorot's CEO notes Israel's capacity to "unify various water sources into a single system"—but this unification is operational, not constitutional. The system is technically integrated but governmentally subordinate.

This distinction is critical. Mekorot demonstrates that centralized water intelligence is technically feasible and operationally superior to fragmented management. But it does not demonstrate constitutional resource governance. The CHG framework builds on Mekorot's operational model while adding the constitutional layer that Mekorot lacks: legally specified safe operating spaces, machine-enforceable viability constraints, democratic accountability mechanisms, and a formal failure doctrine. The comparison is not competitive but architectural: Mekorot represents the sensing and operational optimization layer; CHG adds the constitutional cognition and enforcement layer.

