CHAPTER 9: ADVERSARIAL HYDROLOGY — FAILURE MODES AND LAYERED DEFENSE

9.1 The Adversarial Turn in Water Governance

Water infrastructure has become a cyber-physical target. The 2025 Norwegian dam cyberattack—where attackers gained remote access to dam control systems and opened a valve—demonstrated the catastrophic potential of unsecured water infrastructure. Research on "baiting AI" explores "a new cyber-attack vector targeting Industrial Control Systems, particularly focusing on water treatment facilities". The convergence of digitized water infrastructure, AI-driven control systems, and geopolitical instability creates a threat surface that conventional water governance has not contemplated.

The CHG architecture must therefore be adversarially robust. This is not merely a technical cybersecurity requirement; it is a constitutional requirement. If the AI system that enforces sustainability constraints can be compromised, the constitutional protections it embodies are void. The adversarial governance framework is organized around four threat categories and their corresponding defense mechanisms.

9.2 Cyber-Physical Threats and Byzantine-Robust Sensing

Attack vectors include SCADA intrusion (compromising the industrial control systems that operate pumps, valves, and treatment plants), sensor spoofing (injecting false data into the telemetry stream to cause the AI to make erroneous allocation decisions), data poisoning (corrupting the training data for machine learning models, causing them to learn systematically biased policies), and optimization sabotage (manipulating the CMDP's reward function or constraint parameters).

Defense mechanisms operate at multiple layers. Byzantine-robust state estimation aggregates data from multiple, potentially compromised sensors to maintain an accurate estimate of the hydric state vector even when a minority of sensors are reporting false data. Anomaly detection systems, trained on normal operational patterns, flag deviations that may indicate an attack. Air-gapped fallback controllers maintain a physically isolated backup system that can assume control if the primary AI layer is compromised.

Morocco's ONEE platform targets IEC 62443 compliance, which provides a baseline cybersecurity framework for industrial automation and control systems. The CHG architecture extends this baseline with AI-specific defenses: adversarial training of machine learning models (exposing them to simulated attacks during training to improve robustness), formal verification of critical control policies (proving that certain safety properties hold even under specified attack models), and a "human-in-the-loop" requirement for any allocation decision that would cause the hydric state to approach a constitutional constraint boundary.

9.3 Algorithmic Failure Modes: Goodhart's Law and Model Drift

Goodhart's Law states that "when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure." In the context of CHG, this means that if the AI is trained to optimize a specific sustainability metric—say, the three-year rolling average of groundwater levels—strategic agents (or the AI itself) may find ways to satisfy the metric while degrading the underlying objective. For example, the AI might allocate water in a pattern that keeps the three-year average above the threshold while causing a precipitous decline in year four, or strategic farmers might temporarily reduce extraction during the measurement window while increasing it at other times.

Model drift occurs when the learned allocation policy becomes progressively suboptimal or unsafe as climate conditions, crop markets, or farmer behavior change in ways not represented in the training data. In non-stationary environments—which climate change guarantees—any fixed policy will eventually drift out of its validity domain. The AI's predictions become increasingly unreliable, and its allocation decisions increasingly misaligned with constitutional constraints.

Defense mechanisms include multi-metric monitoring: tracking a diverse set of sustainability indicators rather than optimizing a single metric. Out-of-distribution detection algorithms flag states for which the model's predictions are unreliable, triggering a fallback to rule-based allocation. Regular re-calibration against ground-truth measurements—including satellite-derived groundwater storage anomalies from GRACE/GRACE-FO and InSAR subsidence data—provides an independent check on model accuracy. And the constitutional override mechanism ensures that if the AI makes systematically erroneous decisions, human intervention is available.

9.4 Institutional Failure Modes: Capture and Constitutional Suspension

Regulatory capture—where the ANWA becomes dominated by the interests it is supposed to constrain—is a perennial risk for any independent agency. Agricultural elites who consume disproportionate shares of water resources have both the incentive and the capacity to influence the ANWA's governance board, its technical staff, or the political authorities who oversee it.

Constitutional suspension occurs when emergency powers are used to override sustainability constraints permanently rather than temporarily. A severe drought may justify temporarily relaxing aquifer extraction limits; the risk is that the "temporary" relaxation becomes permanent, and the constitutional constraint becomes a dead letter.

AI lock-in occurs when path-dependent investment in a particular algorithmic architecture—specific models, data pipelines, software infrastructure—makes it progressively more costly to change course, even when the architecture is demonstrably underperforming. This is a form of institutional inertia amplified by technological specificity.

Defense mechanisms include staggered board appointments (preventing any single government from capturing the entire ANWA board), mandatory parliamentary review of any emergency override lasting more than 90 days, a constitutional requirement that the AI architecture be periodically reviewed and subject to legislative re-authorization, and transparency obligations that make capture visible: quarterly publication of the value function, constraint satisfaction metrics, and all overrides with their justifications.

9.5 The Challenge Membrane: Democratic Contestability of Algorithmic Decisions

Drawing on emerging work in algorithmic constitutionalism, the CHG architecture includes a "Challenge Membrane" that operationalizes "answerability with bite" for algorithmic decisions. The membrane grants pause rights (affected parties can temporarily halt an allocation decision pending review), bounded response windows (the ANWA must respond to challenges within specified timeframes), and independent escalation (challenges can be escalated to parliamentary committees or administrative courts).

This mechanism addresses the concern—articulated in the algorithmic governance literature—that "increasing delegation of consequential decisions to algorithmic systems that encode value trade-offs between liberty and security, equity and efficiency, and expression and control" occurs "without visibility, legal justification, or institutional oversight". The Challenge Membrane ensures that algorithmic allocation decisions are contestable through due process, preserving democratic accountability within a computationally enforced governance architecture.

