CHAPTER 10: EQUITY, THE PARETO FRONTIER, AND THE GINI CONSTRAINT

10.1 The Distributional Stakes of AI-Governed Allocation

Any water allocation system—AI-governed or not—has distributional consequences. In Morocco, the current allocation regime produces systematic inequality: export-oriented agribusiness (approximately 15% of farmers) consumes approximately 65% of irrigation water, while smallholder farmers (approximately 70% of the farming population) consume approximately 25%. An AI system that optimizes for aggregate water productivity—economic output per cubic meter—would, if unconstrained, amplify this inequality, because export-oriented farms generate higher value per cubic meter and would receive priority allocation.

This creates a fundamental tension between efficiency and equity that cannot be resolved algorithmically. It must be resolved constitutionally. The CHG architecture embeds equity as a constitutional constraint—not as an optimization target that can be traded off, but as a hard boundary that cannot be violated regardless of efficiency gains. Specifically, the Gini coefficient of irrigation water access is constrained to remain below a legislatively specified threshold, and the minimum human right to water allocation (per Law 36-15, Article 2) is a non-negotiable hard constraint.

10.2 The Pareto Frontier

The Pareto frontier between aggregate water productivity and distributional equity is computed by varying the weight of the Gini penalty in the CMDP's objective function. At one extreme (no equity constraint), the AI allocates water to maximize aggregate economic output, concentrating allocation among high-value export agriculture and producing maximum inequality. At the other extreme (binding Gini constraint), the AI allocates water more evenly, reducing aggregate output but ensuring that smallholder farmers maintain viable livelihoods and that the constitutional minimum water right is satisfied everywhere.

The Pareto frontier is not presented as a technical solution to the equity-efficiency trade-off. It is presented as a transparency mechanism: by making the trade-off visible and quantifiable, the CHG architecture enables informed democratic deliberation about where on the frontier society should operate. The constitutional constraint—the maximum acceptable Gini coefficient—is a political decision, not an algorithmic one. The AI's role is to enforce that decision efficiently, not to make it.

10.3 The Gini Constraint as Constitutional Safeguard

The Gini coefficient of irrigation water access is formalized as a constitutional constraint:

g_{\text{Gini}}(\mathbf{H}_t, \mathbf{U}_t) = G(\mathbf{a}_t) - G_{\max} \leq 0

where $\mathbf{a}_t$ is the vector of water allocations across farmer categories at time $t$, $G(\cdot)$ is the Gini coefficient function, and $G_{\max}$ is the legislatively specified maximum acceptable inequality. This constraint is embedded in the CMDP's barrier functions, meaning that any allocation policy that would cause the Gini coefficient to exceed $G_{\max}$ is infeasible.

This mechanism addresses the concern—articulated by critics of algorithmic governance—that "AI will marginalize the rural poor." The Gini constraint ensures that even if the AI's unconstrained optimization would produce extreme inequality, the constitutional constraint prevents it. The constraint is transparent: parliament can see what the Gini threshold is, what the actual Gini coefficient is, and whether the constraint is binding. If parliament wishes to change the threshold—trading off more equity for less efficiency, or vice versa—it can do so through ordinary legislative processes. The AI simply enforces whatever threshold is democratically specified.

