CHAPTER 11: FROM HYDRAULIC TO COGNITIVE INFRASTRUCTURE — THE INSTITUTIONAL BLUEPRINT

11.1 The Autonomous National Water Authority (ANWA)

The ANWA is designed as a constitutionally autonomous institution, analogous to an independent central bank but governing a different existential resource. Its mandate is specified in the Strategic Water Constitution: to maintain the national hydric state vector within the safe operating space $\mathcal{S}$, to allocate water across sectors in accordance with constitutional priorities, and to publish quarterly transparency reports including the value function, constraint satisfaction metrics, and all executive overrides with justifications.

The ANWA's governance board consists of seven members appointed for staggered seven-year terms: two appointed by the executive, two by parliament, two by an independent nominating commission drawn from professional hydrological and legal bodies, and one chair appointed jointly. The staggered terms and mixed appointment authority are designed to prevent any single political actor from capturing the board and to ensure institutional continuity across electoral cycles.

The ANWA's technical operations are organized around the National Cognitive Water Architecture (NCWA), a layered system that separates sensing, prediction, and actuation to prevent any single entity from controlling the full governance loop. The sensing layer is owned and operated by ONEE, which provides the single source of truth for infrastructure telemetry. The prediction and allocation layer is the scientific core, implemented as a research-grade computational engine that feeds from ONEE data and satellite sources, computes allocation recommendations respecting constitutional constraints, and monitors its own failure modes. The actuation layer is the institutional innovation: ANWA converts algorithmic outputs into binding legal directives, maintains parliamentary transparency, and provides constitutionally defined human-override procedures.

11.2 The Strategic Water Constitution: Key Articles

Article 1 (Hydric Sovereignty): The water resources of the Kingdom of Morocco constitute a strategic national asset. Their long-term sustainability shall be constitutionally guaranteed through the mechanisms established in this Constitution.

Article 2 (Safe Operating Space): The National Water Authority shall maintain the national hydric system within a safe operating space defined by the following constraints: (a) no basin's rolling three-year groundwater decline shall exceed 10% of mean renewable recharge; (b) reservoir storage shall remain above dead pool with ≥95% probability over a five-year horizon; (c) the minimum human right to water allocation (80 liters per person per day) shall be maintained in all regions; (d) the Gini coefficient of irrigation water access shall not exceed $G_{\max}$, as periodically reviewed by parliament.

Article 3 (Autonomy): The National Water Authority shall exercise its mandate independently. No public authority shall instruct the Authority to take actions that would cause the hydric system to exit the safe operating space.

Article 4 (Override): The Prime Minister may, in a declared state of water emergency, override a specific allocation decision of the Authority. Such override shall trigger automatic parliamentary review within 30 days, mandatory public disclosure of the hydric consequences, and a requirement that the override be compensated through alternative water supply measures within the fiscal year.

Article 5 (Transparency): The Authority shall publish quarterly reports including the current hydric state vector, the value function and constraint satisfaction metrics, all allocation decisions, and all overrides with their justifications. These reports shall be subject to independent audit by the Court of Accounts.

Article 6 (Periodic Review): The computational architecture of the National Cognitive Water Architecture shall be reviewed every five years by an independent scientific panel appointed by parliament. Parliament may, on the recommendation of this panel, direct the Authority to modify or replace its algorithmic systems.

11.3 The Delegation Game: When Will Politicians Cede Authority?

The Bayesian delegation game predicts that political elites will rationally delegate enforcement authority to ANWA when three conditions jointly hold: (i) drought severity is high enough that the probability of a visible water crisis exceeds the probability of elite backlash from constrained extraction; (ii) elite asset exposure to water-dependent sectors is sufficiently diversified that the long-term value of system stability exceeds the short-term value of unrestricted extraction; and (iii) constitutional commitment devices are credible enough that delegation is perceived as binding on future governments.

Calibrated against Morocco's 2020–2027 National Drinking Water Program, the game suggests that the delegation window may be opening. The 2025–2026 centralization of water data under ONEE's platform demonstrates a trend toward centralized governance. The investment of 130 billion dirhams (USD 13 billion) in water and energy infrastructure by 2030 indicates that the state has recognized the strategic nature of the water challenge. The seven consecutive years of drought have made the probability of crisis salient to both elites and the public. The remaining condition is the credibility of constitutional commitment devices—and this is precisely what the Strategic Water Constitution is designed to provide.

