CHAPTER 12: SCALABILITY AND GENERALIZABILITY

12.1 From Souss-Massa to National Scale

The CHG architecture is designed for scalability. The hierarchical structure—a national Water Governor setting basin-level caps, basin agents allocating within those caps—maps naturally onto the institutional geography of Moroccan water governance. The Souss-Massa basin serves as the minimum viable product; once validated, the architecture scales by replicating the basin-level CMDP for additional basins (Draa, Oum Er-Rbia, Sebou, Bouregreg) while retaining the national coordination layer.

The computational requirements scale linearly with the number of basins, not exponentially, because basin agents operate largely independently within their national caps. The main computational bottleneck is the national agent's constrained optimization, which must solve a higher-dimensional CMDP as more basins are added. However, the national agent operates on a coarser temporal and spatial resolution (quarterly basin caps rather than daily field-level allocations), making the problem tractable for foreseeable computational resources.

12.2 Generalizability to Other Semi-Arid States

The CHG framework is generalizable to any semi-arid state confronting structural hydric deficit, subject to four institutional preconditions: (i) centralized state capacity sufficient to establish and enforce a constitutional water authority; (ii) digital infrastructure sufficient to support real-time monitoring (satellite data can substitute for sparse ground-based sensors); (iii) climate pressure sufficient to make the political costs of inaction exceed the political costs of delegation; and (iv) manageable geographic scale such that the computational requirements of the national CMDP are tractable.

Jordan, Tunisia, and Sahel states are candidates for CHG adoption, though each faces institutional constraints that would require contextual adaptation. The core theoretical contribution—constitutional computability—is generalizable beyond water to any existential resource commons where sustainability constraints can be formalized as machine-enforceable viability conditions. Carbon budgets, fisheries, and biodiversity targets are all candidates for constitutional computability. Water is the appropriate starting point because it is the most immediately binding constraint on human security in the climate era.

