ANNEX D: COMPARATIVE CASE PROFILES

D1. Aral Sea — Terminal Irrigation Collapse Under Unconstrained Central Planning

Geography: Central Asia (Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan). Once the world’s fourth‑largest inland sea (68,000 km²).

Collapse Trajectory: Soviet central planning diverted the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers to irrigate cotton monocultures in the Central Asian deserts, causing the Aral Sea to lose 90% of its volume between 1960 and 2010. Salinity tripled; commercial fisheries collapsed; exposed seabed became a toxic dust source causing severe public health impacts across the region.

Governance Mechanism: Production quotas were binding; hydrological limits were not. The planning apparatus had no constitutional or computational mechanism for monitoring and enforcing a water balance constraint. Post‑Soviet fragmentation produced continued depletion under multiple national jurisdictions, and international coordination efforts (e.g., the International Fund for Saving the Aral Sea) have been “largely ineffective.”

Relevance for CHG: The Aral Sea is the archetypal case of hydric system death under total state control without biophysical constraint feedback. Centralization is necessary but insufficient; it must be constitutionally constrained by the hydrological realities it governs. The CHG framework directly addresses this failure mode: constitutional constraints are machine‑enforceable and overrideable only through politically costly procedures. Lesson: Constitutional computability—embedding biophysical limits as hard allocation constraints—is the structural response to unconstrained central planning.

D2. Ogallala Aquifer (US High Plains) — Managed Depletion Under Sophisticated Institutions

Geography: Underlies eight U.S. states (South Dakota to Texas), supporting a USD 35 billion agricultural economy.

Depletion Trajectory: Extraction consistently exceeds natural recharge across most of the aquifer’s extent. Parts of the aquifer in New Mexico have been depleted by an average of approximately 16 feet since predevelopment. Large areas of the southern High Plains are projected to become uneconomical for irrigation within 30 years.

Governance Mechanism: Local management districts exist and scientific monitoring is extensive, but extraction decisions are decentralized across thousands of individual irrigators. Groundwater law in most overlying states follows variations of the “reasonable use” or “prior appropriation” doctrines, neither of which imposes a binding sustainability constraint. Voluntary conservation programs have been proposed, including financial incentives to idle groundwater use, but remain fragmented and insufficient. As groundwater law scholarship recognizes, “without such incentives, each user of the commons will continue to overuse her share.”

Relevance for CHG: The Ogallala demonstrates that even sophisticated legal systems with scientific monitoring can produce “managed depletion” when local governance incentives favor current extraction over long‑term sustainability. The collective action problem is individually rational but collectively catastrophic. Lesson: A hierarchical architecture where constitutional constraints at the state or federal level override local extraction pressure—subject to democratic override—is the structural response. CHG’s national‑level cap with basin‑level allocation directly addresses this governance gap.

D3. Cape Town — Anticipatory Failure and the Day Zero Crisis (2017‑2018)

Geography: Western Cape, South Africa. Mediterranean climate, winter rainfall. Population approximately 4.6 million (metro).

Crisis Trajectory: Following three consecutive dry years (2015–2017), dam storage fell to 13% of capacity in early 2018. The municipality prepared for “Day Zero”—the point at which municipal supply would be cut off and residents would queue for daily water rations. The crisis was averted by aggressive demand reduction (a 50% cut, from 1,200 to 600 million litres per day) and fortuitous late‑season rainfall.

Governance Mechanism: The city possessed sophisticated hydrological models and had implemented demand management programs, but “consistently failed to cut demand below the 500‑million‑litre‑per‑day citywide target.” Mandatory restrictions were delayed until the crisis was undeniable, by which point the margin for error had vanished. The failure was anticipatory: the governance system could model the risk but could not act pre‑emptively because restriction decisions were politically mediated.

Relevance for CHG: Cape Town demonstrates that physical scarcity is amplified by governance that cannot forecast and act before crisis. The CHG framework would have triggered mandatory restrictions when the probability of dam storage reaching dead pool crossed a pre‑specified constitutional threshold—removing the political incentive to delay. Lesson: Pre‑commitment through constitutional constraint, with politically costly override, enables anticipatory action that political discretion systematically delays.

D4. Jordan — Chronic Aquifer Stress, Fragmented Governance, and Illegal Water Markets

Geography: Eastern Mediterranean. 61 m³/capita/year of renewable freshwater (absolute scarcity threshold: 500 m³).

Depletion Trajectory: Groundwater provides 58% of national water needs but has been “heavily depleted due to excessive pumping.” Surface water faces sharp seasonal fluctuations linked to climate change. Despite billions of dollars in infrastructure investment, water policies are “not delivering on their promises.”

Governance Mechanism: Institutional responsibility is fragmented across multiple agencies with overlapping mandates. The sectoral approach fails to recognize “the interdependence of water uses” and does not promote “a holistic approach that balances human needs with environmental protection.” An illegal water market has emerged, fed by “predominantly illegal water abstractions,” often transported by tanker trucks. This parallel market is economically rational but hydrologically catastrophic, accelerating aquifer depletion without any sustainability constraint.

Relevance for CHG: Jordan represents the endpoint of ungoverned hydric depletion in a semi‑arid state. Governance fragmentation creates a vacuum that illegal markets fill. Lesson: A constitutional AI architecture that unifies water allocation under a single, legally constrained institution—with satellite‑based monitoring capable of detecting illegal abstractions—could in principle eliminate the governance vacuum. The question is whether Jordan possesses the institutional preconditions (centralized state capacity, digital infrastructure, manageable scale) for CHG adoption.

D5. Israel / Mekorot — Centralized Operational Intelligence Without Constitutional Autonomy (Benchmark, Not Failure)

System Description: Israel’s National Water Carrier, operational since 1964, and the national water company Mekorot provide the closest existing approximation to centralized water intelligence. Israel achieves <4% water loss (lowest globally), reuses nearly 90% of wastewater, and has integrated five large desalination plants into a unified national grid. Mekorot’s AI systems optimize pump scheduling, leak detection, and energy arbitrage. Its VP of Technology has articulated a vision of “near‑total autonomy within the next decade.” The system’s robustness was demonstrated on 7 October 2023, when automated backup systems maintained uninterrupted water supply.

Governance Mechanism: Mekorot is a state‑owned utility operating under executive authority. Its AI systems optimize operational parameters within a policy framework set by political authorities. They do not set sustainability constraints, allocate across sectors through legally binding mechanisms, or provide constitutional accountability.

Relevance for CHG: Israel demonstrates that centralized, AI‑augmented water management is technically feasible and operationally superior to fragmented management. But it does not demonstrate constitutional resource governance. CHG builds on Mekorot’s operational model while adding the constitutional layer: legally specified safe operating spaces, machine‑enforceable viability constraints, democratic accountability, and a formal failure doctrine. Lesson: Sensing and operational optimization are necessary but not sufficient. Constitutional cognition completes the governance architecture.

D6. California SGMA — Legal Mandate Without Computational Enforcement (Ongoing Experiment)

Legislation: Sustainable Groundwater Management Act (2014), requiring high‑ and medium‑priority basins to achieve sustainable yield by 2040–2042 through locally developed Groundwater Sustainability Plans.

Implementation: 266 Groundwater Sustainability Agencies with heterogeneous technical capacity and competing interests. Plans must include data collection and public reporting. Emerging pilot projects use remote sensing and machine learning to estimate pumping, but enforcement remains contentious and uneven.

Relevance for CHG: SGMA proves that a legal mandate without computational enforcement architecture produces uneven compliance. It represents the most ambitious effort to legislate groundwater sustainability in a democratic federation, but its reliance on local planning without machine‑enforceable constraints limits its effectiveness. Lesson: A constitutional AI architecture must couple legal constraint with machine‑enforceable viability conditions, not merely aspirational planning subject to local interpretation and political negotiation.

